Terraform is an open-source infrastructure-as-code tool that lets you define infrastructure in configuration files, rather than writing imperative step-by-step procedures. In Terraform, you describe the desired end state, and Terraform works out what needs to change to reach it. This declarative approach makes infrastructure changes more reproducible and easier to review.

Terraform configurations are written in HashiCorp Configuration Language, commonly called HCL. Within HCL, you typically declare resources and other building blocks such as data sources, variables, outputs, locals, provider settings, and modules. A resource represents an infrastructure object that Terraform manages, such as a storage bucket, a network component, or an application-related service. Terraform does not directly “run” infrastructure itself; instead, it uses a provider to translate resource operations into calls to an external platform API.

A provider is the integration layer that supplies the implementations for resources and data sources for a target system such as AWS, Azure, Kubernetes, GitHub, or a DNS service. When you run Terraform, it compares your configuration with Terraform state and observed infrastructure to calculate the changes required. Terraform also builds a dependency graph so it can order resource creation and updates correctly, which helps avoid failures caused by missing prerequisites.

Dependencies can be inferred automatically when one resource references another, which is common for network-to-compute relationships. If inference is insufficient, you can add an explicit depends_on relationship to enforce ordering. This core model supports reviewable changes, repeatable environments, and shared infrastructure conventions.

Example 1 shows a minimal HCL configuration that creates one provider-supported resource, an AWS S3 bucket, using a provider and a single resource block.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-terraform-core-model-example-bucket"
}
```

Example 2 illustrates a concrete dependency pattern where a compute resource depends on a network resource by referencing it, so Terraform can order creation using the dependency graph.
